---
// src/pages/blog/adhesive-bra-spec-guide.astro
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../../components/Picture.astro';
import BlogSchema from '../../components/BlogSchema.astro';
import { blogSchemaBySlug } from '../../data/blog-schema';

const entry = blogSchemaBySlug['adhesive-bra-spec-guide'];
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Adhesive Bra Manufacturing: What Buyers Get Wrong in the Spec"
  description="Sticky bras fail for spec reasons, not user error - adhesive chemistry, edge thickness, cup moulding and wash survival, from a factory that builds them."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Adhesive Bra Spec Guide</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark mb-8">
      <Picture src="blog-adhesive-spec-hero.webp" alt="Rabbit-ear style adhesive silicone bra shown in two colourways with wing and clasp detail" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" priority sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
    </div>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">November 10, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">Adhesive Bra Manufacturing: What Buyers Get Wrong in the Spec</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        Sticky bras have the worst return rate of any category we run. Not because the product is
        difficult to make well, but because most spec sheets describe the wrong things. They list a
        colour, a size range and a target price, then leave the four variables that actually decide
        whether the bra holds on day three to the factory's default.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are the four we now insist on writing down before a single cup goes into mould.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Adhesive chemistry, not "strong glue"</h2>
      <p>
        Every buyer asks for strong adhesive. Almost none specify which type, and the difference
        between them is the difference between a 25-wear product and a 6-wear product.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Acrylic pressure-sensitive.</strong> The default. Cheap, works immediately, releases cleanly. But it degrades with body heat and skin oil, so tack drops noticeably within ten wears.</li>
        <li><strong>Silicone gel adhesive.</strong> Higher cost, softer hand-feel, and it re-tacks after washing far better than acrylic. This is what separates a reorder from a one-off purchase.</li>
        <li><strong>Hybrid layered adhesive.</strong> A silicone skin-contact layer over an acrylic carrier. Best balance we run: comfortable on the skin, keeps the bond economics sane.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When a quote comes in 40% under another, the adhesive layer is usually the first thing that
        changed. Ask which chemistry the quote assumes before comparing a single number.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Edge thickness — where every peel starts</h2>
      <p>
        The perimeter is the whole game. A thick edge gives you a sharp step against the skin, and
        that step is what lifts first when the wearer moves. Once a 5 mm section lifts, the peel
        travels inward and the bra is off.
      </p>
      <p>
        Specify a tapered edge — we run ours down to roughly 0.8–1.2 mm at the outer rim — and specify
        that the taper is moulded, not trimmed. Trimmed edges are faster and they are the single most
        common source of edge lift complaints in feedback.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Cup moulding and the push-up insert</h2>
      <p>
        A push-up adhesive bra is two products pretending to be one: a moulded silicone shell and an
        internal pad that provides the lift. If they are bonded as an afterthought, the pad migrates
        and the cup deforms in the wash.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ask whether the pad is <strong>encapsulated during moulding</strong> or glued in afterwards. Encapsulated, the pad cannot shift.</li>
        <li>Specify pad thickness by size band. A uniform 8 mm pad across S–XL gives the small sizes a padded look and leaves the large sizes under-supported.</li>
        <li>Ask for the shore hardness of the shell silicone. Softer conforms better; harder holds shape. Full-bust sizes need a firmer shell with a softer adhesive layer, not one uniform material.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>4. Wash survival is a test, not an assumption</h2>
      <p>
        "Reusable" means nothing without a number. We test to a defined cycle count — hand wash, air
        dry, re-apply — and record tack retention at each stage. A spec that says "reusable, 20+ wears"
        and cannot show the test data behind it is a marketing claim, not a specification.
      </p>
      <p>
        Write the test into the purchase order: cycle count, pass threshold (tack retention percentage),
        and the failure definition. That single clause has done more for our return rate than any change
        to the silicone itself.
      </p>

      <h2>What to put on the spec sheet</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Adhesive chemistry type and target tack retention after 20 cycles</li>
        <li>Edge taper thickness at the rim, moulded not trimmed</li>
        <li>Pad encapsulation method and per-size pad thickness</li>
        <li>Shell shore hardness, stated separately from the adhesive layer</li>
        <li>Wash test method and pass/fail threshold written into the PO</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of that adds cost. It just moves the decision from the factory floor to the spec sheet,
        where you can still control it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Sourcing adhesive bras?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone mt-1">We mould silicone cups in-house and test adhesive re-use and heat-ageing before shipping. <a href="/contact" class="text-accent hover:underline">Request the material spec</a> or see our <a href="/products/silicone-bras" class="text-accent hover:underline">silicone bra range</a>.</p>
    </div>
  <BlogSchema slug="adhesive-bra-spec-guide" entry={entry} />

  </article>
</BlogLayout>
